ormulated concretely over the complex numbers as follows. Conjecture 1 (Jacobian Conjecture) Let $latex {F:{\\bf C}^n \\rightarrow {\\bf C}^n}&fg=000000$ …","language":"en","type":"link","author_name":"","author_url":"","provider_name":"What's new","provider_url":"","html":"","width":200,"height":200,"image":null,"image_description":"","embed_url":"","blurhash":"UnNA^y~X%3tRE0xb%MM{RQs;InRj-=WBWBj[","published_at":"2026-07-21T21:04:44.000Z","authors":[]},"poll":null,"quote_approval":{"automatic":["public"],"manual":[],"current_user":"denied"}},{"id":"116958673277338837","created_at":"2026-07-21T15:28:44.569Z","in_reply_to_id":null,"in_reply_to_account_id":null,"sensitive":false,"spoiler_text":"","visibility":"public","language":"en","uri":"https://mathstodon.xyz/ap/users/116091818403192892/statuses/116958673277338837","url":"https://mathstodon.xyz/@alerad/116958673277338837","replies_count":0,"reblogs_count":1,"favourites_count":2,"quotes_count":0,"edited_at":null,"content":"<p>Formalized in Lean4:</p><p>The map F : K³ → K³ has constant Jacobian −2 yet identifies three points. This is no accident of algebra: F is, definitionally, the proof is rfl, the map &quot;binary cubic with a marked simple root ↦ forget the marking&quot;, for the family cT³ − 2T²U + bTU² − 2aU³.</p><p>So every fiber is the set of simple roots of one cubic. The Keller condition says the root you stand on is simple, a local fact. It cannot control how many simple roots the cubic has: 3 off the discriminant hypersurface, 1 on it, 0 on its singular curve (the triple roots), which is exactly the locus the image misses. All machine-checked now, including disc = −4W and &quot;missed curve = singular locus of the discriminant&quot;.</p><p>My favorite part is arithmetic: over any field of char ≠ 2, a fiber can never have exactly two rational points, two roots force a third. Vieta, made constructive.</p><p>The Jacobian Conjecture failed because pointwise simplicity of roots doesn&#39;t count roots.</p><p><a href=\"https://github.com/alerad/alpoge-lean\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" translate=\"no\"><span class=\"invisible\">https://</span><span class=\"\">github.com/alerad/alpoge-lean</span><span class=\"invisible\"></span></a></p><p><a href=\"https://mathstodon.xyz/tags/Lean4\" class=\"mention hashtag\" rel=\"tag\">#<span>Lean4</span></a> <a href=\"https://mathstodon.xyz/tags/Mathlib\" class=\"mention hashtag\" rel=\"tag\">#<span>Mathlib</span></a> <a href=\"https://mathstodon.xyz/tags/JacobianConjecture\" class=\"mention hashtag\" rel=\"tag\">#<span>JacobianConjecture</span></a> <a href=\"https://mathstodon.xyz/tags/FormalVerification\" class=\"mention hashtag\" rel=\"tag\">#<span>FormalVerification</span></a></p>","reblog":null,"application":{"name":"Web","website":null},"account":{"id":"116091818403192892","username":"alerad","acct":"alerad","display_name":"Alejandro Radisic","locked":false,"bot":false,"discoverable":true,"indexable":true,"group":false,"created_at":"2026-02-18T00:00:00.000Z","note":"<p>Learnt math by re-implementing papers; now I build machine-verified mathematics in Lean 4. Creator of LeanCert (certified numerics: proof-producing interval bounds and tactics). Maintainer of the Lean formalization of Alpöge&#39;s 2026 Jacobian-Conjecture counterexample. Contributor to PNT+. Number theory above all.</p>","url":"https://mathstodon.xyz/@alerad","uri":"https://mathstodon.xyz/ap/users/116091818403192892","avatar":"https://media.mathstodon.xyz/accounts/avatars/116/091/818/403/192/892/original/768053f1ce663d85.png","avatar_static":"https://media.mathstodon.xyz/accounts/avatars/116/091/818/403/192/892/original/768053f1ce663d85.png","avatar_description":"","header":"https://mathstodon.xyz/headers/original/missing.png","header_static":"https://mathstodon.xyz/headers/original/missing.png","header_description":"","followers_count":4,"following_count":7,"statuses_count":2,"last_status_at":"2026-08-06","hide_collections":null,"show_media":true,